questions.e:
**Handshake lemma:** every graph has an even number of odd-degree vertices, say $2k$ with $k \ge 1$.

Add a new vertex $v$ adjacent to exactly those $2k$ vertices:
- each previously odd vertex gains one edge, so its degree becomes **even**;
- each previously even vertex is untouched and stays **even**;
- $v$ has degree $2k$, which is **even**.

Every vertex now has even degree, which is the defining condition for an **Euler** graph. (When $G$ is connected, the new graph is connected as well, so it has an Euler circuit.)

The other properties are not guaranteed. Take $G = K_{1,3}$ with centre $c$ and leaves $x, y, z$; all four have odd degree, so $v$ is joined to all of them. The resulting degrees are $c{:}\,4,\ v{:}\,4,\ x, y, z{:}\,2$. The graph is:
- **not regular:** the degrees differ;
- **not complete:** $x$ and $y$ are not adjacent;
- **not Hamiltonian:** each of $x, y, z$ has only $c$ and $v$ as neighbours, so a Hamiltonian cycle would need $c$ to have three neighbours on the cycle.

It is still Eulerian: it is connected and all degrees are even.

**Answer: (D)**

