questions.e:
**Key idea:** check closure, identity and inverses.

- **A: false.** The product of two negative rationals is positive, e.g. $(-1)(-2) = 2$, so the set is not closed. The identity $1$ is not in the set either.
- **B: true.** Take the non-singular $n \times n$ matrices of a fixed order (the general linear group $GL_n$):
  - closure: $\det(AB) = \det A \cdot \det B \neq 0$;
  - associativity: matrix multiplication is associative;
  - identity: $I$ is non-singular;
  - inverses: $\det A \neq 0$ means $A^{-1}$ exists, and $A^{-1}$ is non-singular since $\det(A^{-1}) = 1/\det A$.
- **C: false.** Singular matrices, such as the zero matrix, have no inverse. Matrices of incompatible orders cannot even be multiplied.
- **D: false**, because C is false.

**Answer: (B)**

**Key fact:** DFS of an *undirected* graph produces no cross edges; every non-tree edge $(u,v)$ joins a vertex to one of its **ancestors**. So the new cycle is the tree path between $u$ and $v$ plus the edge $(u,v)$. If the depth difference is $d$, the cycle has $d+1$ edges.

**Difficulty:** we do not know which endpoint is the ancestor, and walking up from the wrong one could go all the way to the root (cost = tree height, not cycle length).

**Fix: climb from both ends in lock-step.** Move $x$ (starting at $u$) and $y$ (starting at $v$) one parent step each per iteration. If $u$ is the ancestor at distance $d$, then $y$ reaches $u$ after exactly $d$ iterations, while $x$ only moves further up, away from $v$, and can never meet $v$ (at the root it just stays, since $P[\text{root}] = \text{root}$). The case "$v$ is the ancestor" is symmetric.

```c
void printCycle(int u, int v)            /* (u,v) is a non-tree edge */
{
    int x = u, y = v, top, bottom, w;
    while (x != v && y != u) {           /* lock-step climb: d iterations */
        x = P[x];
        y = P[y];
    }
    if (y == u) { top = u; bottom = v; } /* u is an ancestor of v */
    else        { top = v; bottom = u; } /* v is an ancestor of u */

    w = bottom;                          /* print tree path bottom -> top */
    while (w != top) {
        printf("%d ", w);
        w = P[w];
    }
    printf("%d %d\n", top, bottom);      /* edge (top,bottom) closes the cycle */
}
```

**Cost:** the climbing loop runs $d$ times and the printing loop $d$ times, so the total time is $O(d) = O(\text{length of the cycle})$, independent of $|V|$ and of the height of $T$.

**Answer: Lock-step parent climbing from $u$ and $v$, then print the ancestor-descendant tree path plus edge $(u,v)$, in $O(\text{cycle length})$ time.**

