An array $A$ contains $n$ integers in locations $A[0], A[1], \ldots A[n-1]$. It is required to shift the elements of the array cyclically to the left by $K$ places, where $1 \le K \le n-1$. An incomplete algorithm for doing this in linear time, without using another array is given below. Complete the algorithm by filling in the blanks. Assume all variables are suitably declared.

```pascal
min:=n;
i=0;
while _____ do
begin
   temp:=A[i];
   j:=i;
   while ____ do
   begin
      A[j]:=____;
      j:=(j+K) mod n;
      if j<min then
         min:=j;
   end;
   A[(n+i-K)mod n]:=____;
   i:=______;
end;
```

questions.e:
**Key idea (juggling / cycle-leader rotation):** after a left shift by $K$, position $j$ must receive the element currently at $(j+K) \bmod n$. Starting at $i$ and repeatedly adding $K$ (mod $n$) traces a cycle of positions. Save $A[i]$ in `temp`, pull each element one step back along the cycle, and finally drop `temp` into the last position of the cycle, $(i-K) \bmod n = (n+i-K) \bmod n$.

- The inner loop must stop just before $j$ would come back to the start $i$: condition `(j+K) mod n <> i` (equivalently `j <> (n+i-K) mod n`), body `A[j] := A[(j+K) mod n]`.
- After the inner loop: `A[(n+i-K) mod n] := temp`.
- The positions split into $g = \gcd(n,K)$ cycles starting at $0, 1, \ldots, g-1$. `min` records the smallest index reached by the inner loop: the cycle of $0$ is $\{0, g, 2g, \ldots\}$, so after it `min` $= g$, and later cycles ($i$ + multiples of $g$) never go lower. Hence the outer loop is `while i < min do` with `i := i+1`.

```pascal
min:=n;
i:=0;
while i < min do
begin
   temp:=A[i];
   j:=i;
   while (j+K) mod n <> i do
   begin
      A[j]:=A[(j+K) mod n];
      j:=(j+K) mod n;
      if j<min then
         min:=j;
   end;
   A[(n+i-K) mod n]:=temp;
   i:=i+1;
end;
```

**Check** ($n=6$, $K=2$, $g=2$): cycle from 0: $A[0]\leftarrow A[2]$, $A[2]\leftarrow A[4]$, $A[4]\leftarrow$ old $A[0]$, and `min` becomes 2. Cycle from 1: $A[1]\leftarrow A[3]$, $A[3]\leftarrow A[5]$, $A[5]\leftarrow$ old $A[1]$. Now $i = 2 \not< 2$, stop. The array is old $A[2], A[3], A[4], A[5], A[0], A[1]$, a left rotation by 2. Every element is moved exactly once: $O(n)$ time, $O(1)$ extra space.

**Answer: `i < min`, `(j+K) mod n <> i`, `A[(j+K) mod n]`, `temp`, `i+1`**

