questions.e:
**(a)** Every row increases to the right and every column increases downward, so $A[1][1]$ is smaller than everything to its right and below it, and by transitivity smaller than every element. Hence $i = 1$, $j = 1$.

**(b) Key idea:** this is a heap-style *sift-down* (the array is a Young tableau). After deleting $A[1][1]$, put $x$ in the hole at $(1,1)$. While $x$ is larger than one of the hole's two successors (below: $A[i+1][j]$, right: $A[i][j+1]$), move the **smaller** successor into the hole and move the hole there. The moved element is smaller than the other successor and larger than the elements above/left of the hole, so the ordering is preserved. When $x$ is smaller than both successors, drop $x$ into the hole.

```pascal
procedure insert (x: integer);
var i,j: integer;
begin
   i:=1; j:=1; A[i][j]:=x;
   while (x > A[i+1][j] or x > A[i][j+1]) do
      if A[i+1][j] < A[i][j+1] then begin
         A[i][j]:=A[i+1][j]; i:=i+1;
      end
      else begin
         A[i][j]:=A[i][j+1]; j:=j+1;
      end;
   A[i][j]:=x
end
```
(The blank in the `if` completes the comparison to `A[i][j+1]`: compare the lower neighbour with the right neighbour.)

**Why it is safe and $O(n)$:** $x$ is smaller than every item of row $n$ and column $n$, so the hole never moves into the last row or column; inside the loop $i, j \le n-1$ and $A[i+1][j]$, $A[i][j+1]$ are valid. Each iteration increases $i + j$ by 1, from 2 up to at most $2n-2$, so there are at most $2n-4$ iterations: $O(n)$.

**Answer: (a) $i=1, j=1$; (b) blanks: `A[i+1][j]`, `A[i][j+1]`, `A[i][j+1]`, `A[i][j]:=A[i][j+1]; j:=j+1`, `x`**

**Working principle – minimax search.** The function evaluates the game tree recursively, depth first:

- A leaf (finished game) is scored directly by `payoff(B)`.
- At a **MAX** node (player 1 to move) `value` starts at $-\infty$ (INF negated) and becomes the **maximum** of the values of all children, each child being evaluated with MIN to move.
- At a **MIN** node (player 2 to move) `value` starts at $+\infty$ and becomes the **minimum** over the children, each evaluated with MAX to move. (-INF and +INF are the identity values for max and min respectively, so the first child's value always replaces them.)
- The value returned for the root is the payoff player 1 can *guarantee* when both players play optimally; the best move is the child that attains it.
- At any moment the chain of active calls `search(root) → search(child) → …` is exactly the path from the root to the node being examined, so the tree is never stored explicitly: space is $O(d)$ activation records for depth $d$, but time is $O(b^d)$ for branching factor $b$, because **every** node is visited.

**Reducing the search – alpha–beta pruning.** Carry two bounds down the recursion: $\alpha$ = best value MAX is already assured of on the current path, $\beta$ = best (lowest) value MIN is already assured of. Call `search(root, MAX, -INF, INF)`:

```pascal
function search(B: boardtype; mode: modetype; alpha, beta: real): real;
var C: boardtype; value: real;
begin
  if B is a leaf then return (payoff(B))
  else if mode = MAX then begin
    value := -INF;
    for each child C of board B do begin
      value := max(value, search(C, MIN, alpha, beta));
      alpha := max(alpha, value);
      if alpha >= beta then return (value)    {beta cut-off}
    end;
    return (value)
  end
  else begin
    value := INF;
    for each child C of board B do begin
      value := min(value, search(C, MAX, alpha, beta));
      beta := min(beta, value);
      if beta <= alpha then return (value)    {alpha cut-off}
    end;
    return (value)
  end
end;
```

Once $\alpha \ge \beta$, the remaining children cannot change the root's value (the opponent higher up already has a better alternative), so they are skipped. The root value is the same as plain minimax, but with good move ordering only about $O(b^{d/2})$ nodes are examined instead of $O(b^d)$ – roughly twice the depth for the same effort. Further savings: depth cut-off with a heuristic evaluation function, ordering the most promising moves first, and remembering already evaluated positions.

**Answer: Minimax search of the game tree; reduce it with alpha–beta pruning**

