**Idea (two pointers):** start with the smallest element ($i = 1$) and the largest ($j = n$). Because the array is sorted, a single comparison of $A[i] + A[j]$ with $M$ lets us throw away one of the two ends for good.

```pascal
procedure FindPair;
var i, j : integer; found : boolean;
begin
  i := 1; j := n; found := false;
  while (i <= j) and (not found) do
    if A[i] + A[j] = M then found := true
    else if A[i] + A[j] < M then i := i + 1   { A[i] is too small }
    else j := j - 1;                          { A[j] is too large }
  if found then writeln('i = ', i, ', j = ', j)
  else writeln('no such i, j exist')
end;
```

**Correctness.** Invariant: if some pair $(p, q)$ with $p \le q$ and $A[p] + A[q] = M$ exists, then $i \le p$ and $q \le j$.
- If $A[i] + A[j] < M$: for every $k \le j$, $A[i] + A[k] \le A[i] + A[j] < M$, so $A[i]$ cannot belong to any pair inside the window — increment $i$.
- If $A[i] + A[j] > M$: for every $k \ge i$, $A[k] + A[j] \ge A[i] + A[j] > M$, so $A[j]$ cannot belong to any pair — decrement $j$.

**Time:** each iteration decreases $j - i$ by one, so there are at most $n$ iterations of $O(1)$ work: $O(n)$ time and $O(1)$ extra space. (If $i$ and $j$ must be different positions, use `i < j` as the loop condition.)

Example: $A = [1, 3, 4, 6, 9]$, $M = 13$: $1+9<13 \Rightarrow i=2$; $3+9<13 \Rightarrow i=3$; $4+9=13$ → output $i=3, j=5$.

**Answer: Two-pointer scan from both ends ($i=1$, $j=n$; advance $i$ when the sum is $< M$, retreat $j$ when it is $> M$) — $O(n)$**

